are organized jointly

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.210
with the Engineering Workforce Development

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:35.330
of the Center for Quantum
Networks in Arizona

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:37.620
and the Yale Quantum Institute.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.970
This is the third panel in our series

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:45.120
on focusing to prepare
today on how to get a job

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.540
or how to put all your
chances on your side

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.080
to get a job in academia

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.570
and to become an assistant professor

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:55.420
or have any faculty position.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.780
So we wanted to offer this series

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.230
of professional development panels,

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:02.520
because building quantum technology

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.530
really requires a lot of people
from different backgrounds.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:07.340
We need physicists, electrical engineers,

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.180
computer scientists,
software engineers, chemists.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.530
And it can be a little bit overwhelming

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.640
to consider all the options and
all the careers you can have

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.840
in the field of quantum
science information.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:21.890
So today we'll hear from postdocs

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:24.510
and recent faculty members

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:27.340
that will talk about their experience

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.320
on what it's like to work
in the field of quantum

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.530
