00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:57.490
Okay. Let's kickstart this meeting.

00:07:57.490 --> 00:08:00.750
And we're gonna answer
some of the questions.

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:02.600
And so I think the first
one that comes in mind

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:05.830
when we think about faculty
member is the stress

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:07.890
as postdocs and as senior grad students

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:11.550
of how can I find a faculty position?

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:13.180
And what are the steps?

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:16.730
And so can I have either Shruti or Linran

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:20.830
go over the process of
what are all the steps

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:23.000
that you took to get this position?

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:26.170
And you can answer that from any angle

00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:27.853
that you feel would be helpful.

00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:32.175
Maybe we can start by Shruti.

00:08:32.175 --> 00:08:33.780
- Okay.

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:38.780
So for me, the process was
you basically care about...

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:41.170
You have to keep an eye...

00:08:41.170 --> 00:08:43.870
So for me, I kind of was
looking where I wanted to be.

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:48.374
And so I kept an eye on the hiring market

00:08:48.374 --> 00:08:50.930
in those particular universities.

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:53.340
And also my colleagues
knew that I was looking

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:56.740
so they would forward me any announcements

00:08:57.810 --> 00:08:59.530
made about hiring at their university.

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:01.200
So you basically...

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:02.820
You can be proactive in looking

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:05.170
for open positions at places,

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:07.090
or you can obviously get help

00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:08.960
from your colleagues about this.

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:11.210
And once you, you know,

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:14.820
usually the process starts
around September, October.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:17.870
You kind of know where you
want to go by September,

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:22.400
and then the deadlines
for applications are

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:25.900
around October/December,
sometimes late December.

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:28.360
So you basically prepare
your application process,

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:29.700
which is like your CV.

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:31.930
You have to write a research statement.

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:34.120
Basically, in your research statement,

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:37.340
you want to convey the things

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:40.203
that you want to do in
the next five years or so

00:09:40.203 --> 00:09:41.883
in your research group.

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:45.670
And then you have to write
a teaching statement.

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:48.770
Sometimes you have to
write diversity statements.

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:51.820
And then there's blank;
you just have to wait.

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:53.570
After all the applications are sent,

00:09:53.570 --> 00:09:56.470
then you just have to wait and have faith

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:58.660
that somebody has actually
found your application

00:09:58.660 --> 00:09:59.960
and is reading it.

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:03.690
And eventually you will hear from places.

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:08.219
Sometimes they call you to
have like a phone interview,

00:10:08.219 --> 00:10:11.050
which is usually like maybe 30 minutes.

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:12.830
Sometimes it's even shorter.

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:16.410
And then if you go through that,

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:19.363
then they'll call for
in-person interviews.

00:10:20.733 --> 00:10:22.870
So I had, in some cases,

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:25.373
both phone interviews
and in-person interviews.

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:28.520
And then again there is a blank.

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:31.410
You wait for a long time to hear back

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:33.260
and sometimes you have to ping them

00:10:33.260 --> 00:10:36.907
and be like, "Hey, where is
the application process at?"

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:42.420
So that was my overall experience.

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:43.610
Maybe I missed something.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:45.063
Lin, do you wanna add in?

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:50.010
- Yeah. So I think you probably
covered all the aspects

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:51.810
of the faculty search,

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:54.490
but my experience is a
little bit different.

00:10:54.490 --> 00:10:57.987
I mean, Florian and
Anderson, and also Shruti,

00:10:57.987 --> 00:11:01.340
you mentioned that networking
is actually quite important.

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:04.550
So the reason why I got
this position is actually

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:08.300
I was quite lucky that
when I was a postdoc...

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:11.540
So I was not prepared for a
faculty position at that time.

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:14.230
And I was approached by some colleague

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:16.970
that I knew when I was a graduate student.

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:20.857
And also I got some information
from my advisors and so,

00:11:20.857 --> 00:11:22.470
"Okay. There's a faculty position.

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:24.010
I think that can fit you well.

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:25.635
And why don't you apply?"

00:11:25.635 --> 00:11:29.546
And then I applied, and
that's how I got it.

00:11:29.546 --> 00:11:32.000
And the preparation is quite similar.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.580
So we need to prepare the CV.

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:37.750
You write the research statement
and the teaching statement,

00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:40.900
diversity statement
and all kinds of stuff.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:44.170
And also normally we need
to give like two talks.

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:48.480
One is the research talk about your work.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:53.480
One is like named job talk to
present your research ideas

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:55.530
for your future group.

00:11:57.350 --> 00:12:00.860
And I think most of
them are very important.

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:04.470
And the research talk
presents your capability

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:06.210
and the job talk

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:11.451
will show how you will lead the group

00:12:11.451 --> 00:12:14.000
as an independent researcher.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:17.420
I think that's also very
important, especially now.

00:12:17.420 --> 00:12:19.220
I believe most universities

00:12:19.220 --> 00:12:21.090
when they post the faculty position

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:25.590
they kind of have a idea

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:26.860
what kind of background,

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:29.090
what kind of person they are looking for.

00:12:29.090 --> 00:12:30.920
So if your...

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:37.350
So if your future plan matches their idea,

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:40.030
I think that will be very helpful.

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:40.863
Yeah.

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:45.923
- How many applications...?

00:12:45.923 --> 00:12:47.479
Can you give I guess a ratio of like

00:12:47.479 --> 00:12:49.130
how many applications did you send?

00:12:49.130 --> 00:12:50.540
How many responses did you get?

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:54.420
Because we always hear like
there's more production of PhDs

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:56.570
than positions available.

00:12:56.570 --> 00:12:58.500
Which I think in quantum is

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:00.760
we are in a weird bubble these days.

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:02.780
I calculated that last year

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:06.640
there was 161 faculty positions open

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:09.570
in the US in quantum.

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:11.460
So it's quite a lot for the field.

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:12.920
It's a very hot topic,

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:16.410
but can you give us a ratio
of how many interview...?

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:19.604
Or like how many positions
did you interview for

00:13:19.604 --> 00:13:21.550
and like how many offer...?

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:22.920
You can also keep that confidential.

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:24.940
Just give us like a broad idea

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:27.293
if you don't want to disclose
any of the other things.

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:29.867
So for me-
- [Linran] I...

00:13:29.867 --> 00:13:31.620
- Sorry. No, go ahead Lin.

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:33.146
- No. Shruti, I think you can go ahead,

00:13:33.146 --> 00:13:35.787
because my case is not common.

00:13:35.787 --> 00:13:37.050
- Right. So for me,

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:40.190
I actually went to the job market twice.

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:44.270
One was 2018/2019 round

00:13:44.270 --> 00:13:46.860
and then the other one
was 2019/2020 round.

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:49.793
So in the first round when I applied,

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:54.533
there were much fewer quantum jobs open.

00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:57.230
I think there was...

00:13:58.430 --> 00:13:59.830
I can't remember exactly how many,

00:13:59.830 --> 00:14:03.260
but I think it was like around five-ish

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:04.910
or more universities that I applied to.

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:08.750
But none of them actually said
quantum information in it.

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:10.883
A lot of times they, you know,

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:16.240
send out a more general call.

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:18.610
So unless you have an inside idea

00:14:18.610 --> 00:14:21.640
about what they're exactly
looking for, you don't know.

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:23.800
And I just wanted to get an experience

00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:27.010
like get out the bad interviews before.

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:29.520
And so I was like, "I'm gonna
apply and see how it goes."

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:31.360
So in that round

00:14:32.980 --> 00:14:36.900
I got one interview
and I didn't get a job.

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:38.240
Fortunately, I didn't get a job

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:40.800
so that I could get a job at Yale.

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:42.905
I'm very happy about that.

00:14:42.905 --> 00:14:45.900
So in the second round, the
second year, the 2019/2020 year,

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:48.910
it was like amazing year for quantum.

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:51.980
As Florian said, there
was so many job openings

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:53.250
in our field.

00:14:53.250 --> 00:14:56.620
And again I think I
applied to about eight,

00:14:56.620 --> 00:14:58.703
nine universities or so.

00:15:01.450 --> 00:15:06.040
I think I pretty much
got calls from everybody

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:07.793
except maybe two places.

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:10.810
And so it was a very stressful time,

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:15.080
'cause these job interviews take two days.

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:19.290
At least two or three days,
and then you're basically on

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:21.623
from 8:00 in the morning
to 8:00 in the night.

00:15:23.451 --> 00:15:24.603
And yeah.

00:15:27.530 --> 00:15:29.160
Like I said, I didn't get called

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:31.420
I think from two places, maybe three.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:34.010
I got in-person interviews

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:37.540
and then I got offers from a few places.

00:15:37.540 --> 00:15:40.650
Again, very lucky because I
know that during that time,

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:45.030
many people's offers were
withdrawn due to the pandemic.

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:47.030
Fortunately, that did not happen for me.

00:15:48.340 --> 00:15:50.350
And that's when I landed at Yale.

00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:55.480
- Yeah. My experience is not...

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:59.540
I mean, because I was
approached by some colleague.

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:03.210
So actually in 2018 I just applied here.

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:06.220
The only place I applied
is University of Arizona.

00:16:07.570 --> 00:16:09.120
And luckily I got this position.

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:12.080
And after interview I
found this is a good place.

00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:15.590
And I like the environment
and the research

00:16:15.590 --> 00:16:17.950
and it has all the capabilities

00:16:17.950 --> 00:16:19.550
that I need for my experiments.

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:22.000
So I just decided to join.

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:25.700
But from my experience
talking to other colleagues.

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:26.560
So...

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:29.823
The ratio is not good.

00:16:29.823 --> 00:16:31.400
I mean, but it's not bad.

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:36.400
So I think probably on
average 1/3 will get a call.

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:41.140
And normally, so people,

00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:45.550
university will invite like
around three to eight people

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:49.300
for onsite interview and they
will have a waiting list.

00:16:49.300 --> 00:16:52.350
And if you are in the top three,

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:54.450
then I think you have
a pretty good chance.

00:16:54.450 --> 00:16:56.730
Because I mean, normally like Shruti

00:16:56.730 --> 00:16:59.290
you will get offers from several places

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:01.000
and you can only accept one.

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:04.800
So then for other places
if you decline that offer,

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:09.250
the second person on that list
will be offered the position.

00:17:09.250 --> 00:17:10.083
Right?

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:15.130
So that's why Shruti mentioned
a lot of lengthy times.

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:17.520
So there'll be a lot of waiting time

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:20.523
during the faculty search.

00:17:22.530 --> 00:17:25.120
- We have a question in the Q and A.

00:17:25.120 --> 00:17:27.650
How long did it take for
you to prepare your research

00:17:27.650 --> 00:17:28.760
and teaching statement?

00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:32.580
So in preparing for the interviews

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:35.213
was any aspect of it
particularly challenging?

00:17:37.499 --> 00:17:40.300
- So actually it's not long.

00:17:40.300 --> 00:17:45.300
Normally just one page or
two pages, so not long.

00:17:45.420 --> 00:17:48.610
I mean, but it takes a lot of time

00:17:48.610 --> 00:17:51.730
to prepare that two-page
research statement, I believe.

00:17:51.730 --> 00:17:55.840
And yeah, I think the key is that you need

00:17:55.840 --> 00:17:59.960
to kind of make a connection
to your previous background,

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:03.350
but you need to find a new
angle to distinguish yourself.

00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:04.280
I think that's probably

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:06.130
the most challenging part for myself.

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:12.100
- I was actually trying to
open up my research statement

00:18:12.100 --> 00:18:15.220
to see how long it was,
but I was not fast enough.

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:17.613
For me, it was much longer.

00:18:18.610 --> 00:18:20.580
- I think the question
is not about the size

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:21.613
of the research statement.

00:18:21.613 --> 00:18:23.110
More like-
- How much time.

00:18:23.110 --> 00:18:27.247
But I mean, it depends on also
how long you have to make it.

00:18:27.247 --> 00:18:30.710
But I started preparing
my research statement

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:32.760
like over the summer,

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:35.740
'cause there's a lot of
thought that goes in there.

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:38.480
You have to show them that you are capable

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:43.230
of conducting new research and
support x graduate students

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:44.660
over the next five years.

00:18:44.660 --> 00:18:46.930
So you have to show them that

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:49.860
you're capable of building
this whole research program.

00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:50.980
You're not a postdoc anymore.

00:18:50.980 --> 00:18:52.940
You're not just going to work
on this one little project,

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:56.770
you're gonna like lead a huge group.

00:18:56.770 --> 00:18:58.430
So it takes a lot of time and effort.

00:18:58.430 --> 00:19:01.330
And the teaching statement,

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:04.280
I spent much less time compared
to the research statement.

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:05.610
It is less important than...

00:19:05.610 --> 00:19:09.160
Well, it's not less important,
it's equally important.

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:11.350
But it's usually only two pages,

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:14.620
so it goes a bit faster
then the research statement.

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:17.280
And then you have to write it early on

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:19.650
so that you have time to send it

00:19:19.650 --> 00:19:21.080
to your colleagues, your advisor,

00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:22.820
to get feedback from them.

00:19:22.820 --> 00:19:24.850
And you know...

00:19:24.850 --> 00:19:26.440
So basically I would say

00:19:27.900 --> 00:19:30.540
by September and at
least it should be ready.

00:19:30.540 --> 00:19:32.780
And you should already be getting feedback

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:33.883
from your colleagues.

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:36.143
- Thank you both.

00:19:37.007 --> 00:19:38.330
Linran, you were talking about the fact

00:19:38.330 --> 00:19:40.380
that your case was a little bit spacial,

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:42.140
because you sort of got picked up

00:19:42.140 --> 00:19:43.287
by one of your colleagues.

00:19:43.287 --> 00:19:45.520
And so I would like to
talk about the importance

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:48.313
of networking and
connection in this field.

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:52.083
How did you start building your network?

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:54.990
And then was he like your direct colleague

00:19:54.990 --> 00:19:57.050
or was it like a more extended network

00:19:57.050 --> 00:19:59.523
of researchers and fellow colleagues?

00:20:01.170 --> 00:20:03.070
- So it's more extended network.

00:20:03.070 --> 00:20:04.130
So because...

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:08.130
Quantum information is
special within the US.

00:20:09.570 --> 00:20:11.470
Even though it's fastly growing,

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:14.100
but it's not a huge community.

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:17.630
So I guess most people know
each other in the field.

00:20:17.630 --> 00:20:20.210
And in many cases,

00:20:20.210 --> 00:20:23.390
maybe your advisor
collaborated on a project

00:20:23.390 --> 00:20:26.680
with some professors
in other universities.

00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:29.900
And if the other university
has a faculty opening,

00:20:29.900 --> 00:20:32.110
they will reach out to your advisor

00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:33.960
and also to some institutes like YQI.

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:37.313
So that's how I know this position.

00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:42.230
- And so the reason I was asking
about networking is because

00:20:42.230 --> 00:20:47.230
we in the US and I think in
many universities in the world

00:20:47.450 --> 00:20:49.970
they're starting to build
a postdoctoral association

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:51.440
that helps with the networking.

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:55.520
And I would like to have a
little bit more information

00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:57.790
about the postdoctoral
association with Anderson.

00:20:57.790 --> 00:20:58.977
Can you talk a little bit about YPA

00:20:58.977 --> 00:21:01.620
and your involvement with the association?

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:05.920
And while you talk I'll pull
up the website of the YPN.

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:06.753
- Yeah. Sure.

00:21:06.753 --> 00:21:10.660
So this is quite a young association.

00:21:10.660 --> 00:21:13.950
It was founded around seven years ago,

00:21:13.950 --> 00:21:17.030
but since then it's been
growing quite a lot.

00:21:17.030 --> 00:21:18.970
And in my view,

00:21:18.970 --> 00:21:21.656
it has been transforming the experience

00:21:21.656 --> 00:21:26.656
of postdocs and also associate
research scientists at Yale

00:21:30.067 --> 00:21:34.417
by exactly favoring
networking, community building,

00:21:36.930 --> 00:21:38.680
because these are one

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:43.680
of the main aims of the Yale
Postdoctoral Association.

00:21:45.040 --> 00:21:48.210
In the YPA, we try
always to create a sense

00:21:48.210 --> 00:21:50.540
of community engagement.

00:21:50.540 --> 00:21:55.540
And also we always try to
allow a positive experience

00:21:56.060 --> 00:21:59.160
at the university.

00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:02.110
By, for example, organizing events

00:22:02.110 --> 00:22:06.230
that are focused on
professional development

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:10.890
like seminars and also workshops.

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:13.390
But also social events,

00:22:13.390 --> 00:22:16.900
which due to the pandemic will
actually stop for a while,

00:22:16.900 --> 00:22:20.700
but we have still been
doing that via the internet

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:23.060
by using Zoom, for example.

00:22:23.060 --> 00:22:24.900
And as Florian mentioned,

00:22:24.900 --> 00:22:28.810
we should never underestimate
the power of networking,

00:22:28.810 --> 00:22:32.780
because we may meet someone
from an unrelated field

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:36.130
and that person may
eventually be your connection

00:22:36.130 --> 00:22:40.680
to someone in your field
by many other means;

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:43.754
because the person is
of the same nationality

00:22:43.754 --> 00:22:45.970
of a researcher in your field.

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:49.460
There are many ways for
you to get connected

00:22:49.460 --> 00:22:52.620
indirectly to someone in your field

00:22:52.620 --> 00:22:56.670
by just meeting people
that are from other fields

00:22:56.670 --> 00:23:01.670
that are let's say in the
same level of your career.

00:23:01.840 --> 00:23:04.910
So I guess most of the people here

00:23:07.639 --> 00:23:12.150
they are postdocs wanting to transition

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:16.847
into let's say academic career.

00:23:18.230 --> 00:23:22.230
And by being a member of an association

00:23:22.230 --> 00:23:23.600
there are many benefits.

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:28.600
I have just mentioned the
benefits related to networking,

00:23:29.100 --> 00:23:30.570
which is one of those

00:23:30.570 --> 00:23:35.570
let's say transferable
soft skills that we learn

00:23:37.330 --> 00:23:41.530
via extracurricular activities,

00:23:41.530 --> 00:23:44.480
because it's quite common
to see people focusing a lot

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:45.920
on researching skills.

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:50.920
And they sometimes forget
to strengthen a little bit

00:23:51.370 --> 00:23:54.990
of other skills that they can also develop

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:58.310
like communication skills, networking,

00:23:58.310 --> 00:24:03.270
leadership, time management,
project management.

00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:05.070
All of these soft skills,

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:07.920
they are extremely necessary

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:10.267
for a successful career.

00:24:14.296 --> 00:24:16.510
By being a member of an association,

00:24:16.510 --> 00:24:20.780
you may gain some of these skills

00:24:20.780 --> 00:24:25.330
by taking part of trainings
that such association may offer.

00:24:25.330 --> 00:24:30.330
But if you also join as an
officer of that association,

00:24:30.850 --> 00:24:33.097
you would also have opportunities

00:24:33.097 --> 00:24:38.097
to strengthen such skills by
organizing events yourself,

00:24:39.440 --> 00:24:42.770
by basically being the leader of a group

00:24:42.770 --> 00:24:46.840
that has a project within the association

00:24:46.840 --> 00:24:50.630
that has to be finished
within a defined time.

00:24:50.630 --> 00:24:55.630
So all of these sort of
non-research activities,

00:24:56.020 --> 00:24:58.580
they somehow teach us these skills

00:24:58.580 --> 00:25:03.203
that are extremely helpful as
well in our academic career.

00:25:04.650 --> 00:25:05.483
- Thank you very much.

00:25:05.483 --> 00:25:07.788
And while you talk, I have
the website of the YPA

00:25:08.904 --> 00:25:10.760
and on my screen.

00:25:10.760 --> 00:25:13.250
So it's YPA.Yale.edu.

00:25:13.250 --> 00:25:16.040
And the people in the
University of Arizona

00:25:16.040 --> 00:25:17.360
also have their own website.

00:25:17.360 --> 00:25:18.880
It's a similar association.

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:22.160
It's UApostdocs.weebly.com.

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:26.750
So you can go and check if
you can make new connection.

00:25:26.750 --> 00:25:30.620
And I really agree that all
the work that you would do

00:25:30.620 --> 00:25:32.040
if you're engaged in the leadership

00:25:32.040 --> 00:25:34.230
of the association is
really, really helpful,

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:35.750
because you will start
building connection.

00:25:35.750 --> 00:25:38.680
And also you will talk to leadership.

00:25:38.680 --> 00:25:43.680
And it's easy to talk to people
on your level, your peers.

00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:45.790
But it's also a different thing

00:25:45.790 --> 00:25:49.477
to start having to ask things
to university leadership.

00:25:49.477 --> 00:25:52.460
And I think it's a good
training also for the job talks,

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:54.100
because that's what you're gonna do.

00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:58.020
You're gonna try to
convince above your grade.

00:25:58.020 --> 00:26:01.160
And so I think it's a
pretty important thing.

00:26:01.160 --> 00:26:03.850
And all the skills that you develop,

00:26:03.850 --> 00:26:06.570
maybe we can go back to
Shruti and Linran talking

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:10.520
about what kind of other
skills do you need to have

00:26:10.520 --> 00:26:11.840
to build a research group?

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:15.050
Because I think the more you go

00:26:15.050 --> 00:26:16.810
into faculty, your job,

00:26:16.810 --> 00:26:19.730
your job is not to do the
research as much as you do,

00:26:19.730 --> 00:26:23.240
but like more managing
students and writing grants.

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:27.220
And you have a ton of serving
on panels at the universities

00:26:27.220 --> 00:26:28.570
and other activities.

00:26:28.570 --> 00:26:31.170
Can you talk a little bit more
about these extra activities

00:26:31.170 --> 00:26:34.913
that grad students or postdocs
are not necessarily aware of?

00:26:37.270 --> 00:26:38.220
Who wants to start?

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:41.600
- Yeah. I can just start it.

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:44.100
So yeah, you are right.

00:26:44.100 --> 00:26:48.660
So it's very different to be
a PhD student or even postdoc

00:26:48.660 --> 00:26:51.080
with being a faculty member.

00:26:51.080 --> 00:26:53.940
So when I was a PhD student or postdoc,

00:26:53.940 --> 00:26:57.060
we just need to focus
on one or two projects.

00:26:57.060 --> 00:27:00.540
You just need to think about
how to do experiments better

00:27:00.540 --> 00:27:01.600
and how to...

00:27:03.324 --> 00:27:05.260
So how to finish the experiment.

00:27:05.260 --> 00:27:09.200
But as a faculty member, I
think the biggest change is that

00:27:10.490 --> 00:27:12.670
we don't really have a lot of time digging

00:27:12.670 --> 00:27:14.850
into the details of other projects;

00:27:14.850 --> 00:27:16.620
but on the other hand, we still need

00:27:16.620 --> 00:27:20.010
to provide the big picture,
guide the students.

00:27:20.010 --> 00:27:22.290
Especially when we start a new group.

00:27:22.290 --> 00:27:25.910
So you are the knowledge
people in the group.

00:27:25.910 --> 00:27:27.470
But on the other hand,

00:27:27.470 --> 00:27:28.930
you have the least time

00:27:28.930 --> 00:27:31.800
to dig into the details of the experiment.

00:27:31.800 --> 00:27:36.203
I think that's the biggest
challenge I've faced.

00:27:38.470 --> 00:27:41.840
So I think it's good.

00:27:41.840 --> 00:27:45.870
It's better to balance the
time you work on proposals.

00:27:45.870 --> 00:27:49.740
And also I still reserve
quite amount of time

00:27:49.740 --> 00:27:51.800
doing experiments with the students,

00:27:51.800 --> 00:27:54.300
at least for the first several years.

00:27:54.300 --> 00:27:57.230
So therefore we can
accelerate the process.

00:27:57.230 --> 00:27:59.260
But I think from...

00:27:59.260 --> 00:28:01.760
It might be different
for a theoretical group.

00:28:01.760 --> 00:28:04.800
So I think Shruti can say more about that.

00:28:04.800 --> 00:28:06.500
But for experiment groups,

00:28:06.500 --> 00:28:10.290
I think that I treat
myself as a half postdoc

00:28:10.290 --> 00:28:11.590
at least for the first year.

00:28:11.590 --> 00:28:15.053
So I still do a lot of like experiments.

00:28:15.910 --> 00:28:19.720
I went into the clean room
with the students together.

00:28:19.720 --> 00:28:23.970
Just help them do the fabrication,
do all the experiments.

00:28:23.970 --> 00:28:26.780
But after three years

00:28:26.780 --> 00:28:30.340
when the first student or
two students are well-trained

00:28:30.340 --> 00:28:31.240
and they are good enough,

00:28:31.240 --> 00:28:34.273
then I can spend more time on other stuff.

00:28:36.410 --> 00:28:38.410
- Yeah. My sentiment is the same thing.

00:28:38.410 --> 00:28:41.840
I always say that having a research group

00:28:41.840 --> 00:28:43.500
is like having a startup.

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:45.620
And so basically whatever skills you need

00:28:45.620 --> 00:28:47.460
to have a startup is what you need.

00:28:47.460 --> 00:28:51.710
You need to be able to write
grants and bring in money.

00:28:51.710 --> 00:28:53.060
You have to support your students.

00:28:53.060 --> 00:28:55.340
You do get a startup package,

00:28:55.340 --> 00:28:58.500
but that's not gonna last you forever.

00:28:58.500 --> 00:29:02.320
And so the advice I was given was like,

00:29:02.320 --> 00:29:03.990
save that money as much as possible

00:29:03.990 --> 00:29:06.800
and just start writing
grants from day one,

00:29:06.800 --> 00:29:08.303
which is what I did.

00:29:10.510 --> 00:29:12.061
It's not only that.

00:29:12.061 --> 00:29:15.250
As Linran also said, you have...

00:29:17.526 --> 00:29:19.390
You have to prepare for your classes.

00:29:19.390 --> 00:29:21.190
You have to prepare your new courses

00:29:21.190 --> 00:29:23.490
that you're gonna teach,
you're gonna write grants,

00:29:23.490 --> 00:29:27.140
so you don't yourself have
a lot of time for research.

00:29:27.140 --> 00:29:28.950
So fortunately in my case

00:29:30.840 --> 00:29:35.210
right in the beginning I had
a student who joined my group.

00:29:35.210 --> 00:29:39.080
And she was pretty much at a good place.

00:29:39.080 --> 00:29:42.050
But I started like a
quite different project

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:44.040
with different set of skillsets for her.

00:29:44.040 --> 00:29:46.140
But she caught on really fast.

00:29:46.140 --> 00:29:50.833
And so you need to be able
to identify, you know,

00:29:51.750 --> 00:29:54.850
fast learners, I guess, and take them on.

00:29:54.850 --> 00:29:59.170
And like right now she is
so good that I don't...

00:29:59.170 --> 00:30:01.970
Basically she's training
the new students coming in.

00:30:01.970 --> 00:30:05.260
So she is really, you know, the...

00:30:05.260 --> 00:30:07.100
I would say I could not have...

00:30:08.240 --> 00:30:10.480
I could take more students on without her.

00:30:10.480 --> 00:30:11.670
I would not have the time to deal

00:30:11.670 --> 00:30:14.070
with everybody at that level.

00:30:14.070 --> 00:30:18.067
The other thing is again like
hiring postdocs and stuff.

00:30:18.067 --> 00:30:19.828
You need to be able to judge them.

00:30:19.828 --> 00:30:22.770
Like when you're interviewing them

00:30:22.770 --> 00:30:24.500
and they come give talks to you,

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:26.620
you need to be able to
ask the right questions

00:30:26.620 --> 00:30:28.970
and judge their capability.

00:30:28.970 --> 00:30:32.760
Again hiring the right people
is going to change your life.

00:30:32.760 --> 00:30:35.500
Like I hired a postdoc who's good.

00:30:35.500 --> 00:30:36.593
I don't have to...

00:30:36.593 --> 00:30:40.820
I just tell him like, we
are thinking about this.

00:30:40.820 --> 00:30:42.820
Go figure it out, and he figures it out.

00:30:43.880 --> 00:30:45.890
Right. So after a while you need it

00:30:45.890 --> 00:30:47.630
to be a self-sustaining thing.

00:30:47.630 --> 00:30:51.750
You cannot be person who's
actually writing codes,

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:54.790
or figuring out the math and stuff.

00:30:54.790 --> 00:30:55.963
So these are the skillsets

00:30:55.963 --> 00:30:59.623
that you don't necessarily
have as a postdoc,

00:31:00.480 --> 00:31:03.883
but, you know, you need to develop.

00:31:04.879 --> 00:31:07.230
- Is there any place that...?

00:31:07.230 --> 00:31:08.470
Like how did you learn this skill?

00:31:08.470 --> 00:31:09.930
Is it on the job?

00:31:09.930 --> 00:31:13.020
Like first day it just
pushed you in the deep end.

00:31:13.020 --> 00:31:13.920
It was like, okay.

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:16.020
You used to be a postdoc,
now you need to interview.

00:31:16.020 --> 00:31:18.180
You need to write grants
and all these things.

00:31:18.180 --> 00:31:20.710
Is there any resources
somewhere that helped you?

00:31:20.710 --> 00:31:22.430
- I mean, I would say that ask...

00:31:22.430 --> 00:31:23.390
Especially for grants.

00:31:23.390 --> 00:31:26.300
I mean, like judging
somebody's capabilities,

00:31:26.300 --> 00:31:27.400
how do you teach that?

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:30.580
You just like...

00:31:30.580 --> 00:31:33.870
For me, it's like observe
when people are giving talks

00:31:33.870 --> 00:31:38.160
and hear what your advisors are
saying about those speakers.

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:39.450
So you know.

00:31:39.450 --> 00:31:41.720
Just catch on to things
that they're saying,

00:31:41.720 --> 00:31:43.350
so that's how you learn.

00:31:43.350 --> 00:31:45.010
Also, ask.

00:31:45.010 --> 00:31:46.330
In my case as a postdoc,

00:31:46.330 --> 00:31:49.753
I did not get a lot of chance
to be involved in grants.

00:31:50.850 --> 00:31:53.060
I got a chance to be
involved in one grant,

00:31:53.060 --> 00:31:54.640
but not you know...

00:31:54.640 --> 00:31:57.750
Mostly for the scientific
part, not so much the writing.

00:31:57.750 --> 00:32:00.350
But I would suggest that
if there's one thing

00:32:00.350 --> 00:32:03.970
I could go back and redo again
would be ask my advisors,

00:32:03.970 --> 00:32:05.330
can I be part of this grant?

00:32:05.330 --> 00:32:08.570
So I learn the procedure and I get

00:32:11.610 --> 00:32:14.370
some training of how these things work,

00:32:14.370 --> 00:32:18.017
how you should present your
work, and so on and so forth.

00:32:19.759 --> 00:32:21.580
- And speaking of grants,

00:32:21.580 --> 00:32:25.280
I know a lot of universities
are giving classes

00:32:25.280 --> 00:32:27.100
and resources, and there's grant writing.

00:32:27.100 --> 00:32:29.930
Are you familiar with this, Anderson?

00:32:29.930 --> 00:32:31.563
Do you want to talk about grant writing

00:32:31.563 --> 00:32:35.920
that can be offered by
the postdoctoral affairs?

00:32:35.920 --> 00:32:40.920
- Yeah. So one thing that
we have been working a lot

00:32:41.500 --> 00:32:44.070
to make sure that we can offer

00:32:44.070 --> 00:32:49.070
to our members is exactly
professional development events,

00:32:50.260 --> 00:32:51.700
especially workshops.

00:32:51.700 --> 00:32:53.870
We have been organizing.

00:32:53.870 --> 00:32:55.820
We have been organizing workshops

00:32:57.328 --> 00:33:01.220
about grant writing, article writing,

00:33:01.220 --> 00:33:04.670
like scientific writing let's say.

00:33:04.670 --> 00:33:08.180
And these are resources that

00:33:08.180 --> 00:33:13.180
we can get out of Yale,
because Yale has many offices

00:33:13.540 --> 00:33:18.540
that organizes different
sorts of workshops.

00:33:20.090 --> 00:33:23.850
They are experts on those specific topics

00:33:23.850 --> 00:33:25.410
and they can teach us,

00:33:25.410 --> 00:33:28.750
but we have to somehow connect with them

00:33:28.750 --> 00:33:30.980
and make such requests.

00:33:30.980 --> 00:33:35.258
And this is something that
we are quite happy to see

00:33:35.258 --> 00:33:40.030
that the YPA has been this very important

00:33:40.030 --> 00:33:43.000
let's say facilitating.

00:33:43.000 --> 00:33:47.900
That such training can

00:33:47.900 --> 00:33:50.863
let's say be offered to our members.

00:33:53.650 --> 00:33:57.070
Because definitely having a method

00:33:57.070 --> 00:34:00.060
for writing grants and papers,

00:34:00.060 --> 00:34:02.740
this makes a huge difference.

00:34:02.740 --> 00:34:05.620
If you know how to structure things first

00:34:05.620 --> 00:34:07.610
before writing that,

00:34:07.610 --> 00:34:10.150
that can speed up the process.

00:34:10.150 --> 00:34:12.320
And the final piece would look

00:34:12.320 --> 00:34:16.160
much more cohesive, for example.

00:34:16.160 --> 00:34:19.130
And these sort of trainings

00:34:19.130 --> 00:34:22.110
we can get out of

00:34:22.110 --> 00:34:27.110
let's say being a member of a
local association of postdocs.

00:34:28.070 --> 00:34:32.510
And if such association does
not exist in your location,

00:34:32.510 --> 00:34:34.760
you could even create your own

00:34:34.760 --> 00:34:38.150
and start things from scratch.

00:34:38.150 --> 00:34:41.000
- And this is what happened to me when...

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:45.030
So I did my PhD in France,
moved to Yale as a postdoc,

00:34:45.030 --> 00:34:47.080
and then there was nobody to help.

00:34:47.080 --> 00:34:50.500
And so a bunch of postdocs that we met

00:34:50.500 --> 00:34:53.930
mostly European because of
the geographical connection.

00:34:53.930 --> 00:34:56.150
We had this mini-network of foreigners

00:34:56.150 --> 00:35:00.180
that met at the visa office at Yale;

00:35:00.180 --> 00:35:01.927
got together and starting to say like,

00:35:01.927 --> 00:35:03.620
"Hey, how can we help each other?"

00:35:03.620 --> 00:35:07.200
Because I think that's also
the trick on everything.

00:35:07.200 --> 00:35:09.560
You should network, you
should help everybody.

00:35:09.560 --> 00:35:11.490
But I think if you find interests,

00:35:11.490 --> 00:35:14.810
like if it's useful to you,
it's gonna be useful to others.

00:35:14.810 --> 00:35:16.320
And so it's something that you can do.

00:35:16.320 --> 00:35:19.340
And we're talking about
Yale and U of A a lot,

00:35:19.340 --> 00:35:21.030
because this is our affiliations.

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:25.670
But this is valid for all
the universities in the US.

00:35:25.670 --> 00:35:28.050
And also in most of the world

00:35:28.050 --> 00:35:30.420
I think the universities
are starting to see

00:35:30.420 --> 00:35:33.171
that people need resources similar to that

00:35:33.171 --> 00:35:36.000
to prep them for the job market.

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:38.080
So they are offering workshops.

00:35:38.080 --> 00:35:41.030
And now with the pandemic,

00:35:41.030 --> 00:35:44.080
I think everybody shifted
to a virtual world.

00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:45.400
And I'm pretty sure even

00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:48.400
if your university doesn't
offer this kind of thing,

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:51.150
you can find virtual
workshops that you can attend,

00:35:51.150 --> 00:35:52.960
or like view recordings online

00:35:52.960 --> 00:35:54.370
like similar to one of these things.

00:35:54.370 --> 00:35:57.400
So it's really something that
you should try to think about,

00:35:57.400 --> 00:35:59.610
because in an ideal world

00:35:59.610 --> 00:36:02.010
you are really good at what
you're doing in your research.

00:36:02.010 --> 00:36:04.060
But the key way you're gonna be judged

00:36:04.060 --> 00:36:05.720
for the applications and for these things

00:36:05.720 --> 00:36:06.780
is gonna be everything else.

00:36:06.780 --> 00:36:08.480
Can you be the extra...?

00:36:08.480 --> 00:36:10.740
Are you the superstar in the field?

00:36:10.740 --> 00:36:11.573
And I think that's what

00:36:11.573 --> 00:36:13.783
people are looking in in universities.

00:36:15.120 --> 00:36:17.000
And so that was one thing.

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:19.280
And also one thing that I want to add.

00:36:19.280 --> 00:36:20.640
We're talking about how to get a job,

00:36:20.640 --> 00:36:22.160
but there's also an interesting thing.

00:36:22.160 --> 00:36:25.320
And I'm sorry I'm gonna bring
the mood a little bit down,

00:36:25.320 --> 00:36:29.487
but not everybody's cut
out to be a faculty member.

00:36:29.487 --> 00:36:32.770
And I think it's very
important to realize like,

00:36:32.770 --> 00:36:33.670
do I want to do that

00:36:33.670 --> 00:36:36.260
because it's the path that you want to do?

00:36:36.260 --> 00:36:38.197
Or is it because your
advisor told you like,

00:36:38.197 --> 00:36:39.210
"Hey, this is what you do.

00:36:39.210 --> 00:36:41.860
You do a PhD, you do a postdoc,
and then you do a faculty."

00:36:41.860 --> 00:36:44.290
And so there's also something
you need to think about:

00:36:44.290 --> 00:36:47.760
is that the kind of job that I want?

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:50.330
And is it matching my skills
and what I want to do?

00:36:50.330 --> 00:36:54.360
And there's a ton of
different jobs in quantum.

00:36:54.360 --> 00:36:58.160
We're gonna cover in the
fall the startup world

00:36:58.160 --> 00:36:59.990
and the industry world.

00:36:59.990 --> 00:37:01.810
I think there's also more and more options

00:37:01.810 --> 00:37:04.730
for a job in the quantum workforce.

00:37:04.730 --> 00:37:07.110
And so if you're scared
about writing grants,

00:37:07.110 --> 00:37:09.450
or if you're scared about
other the things that...

00:37:09.450 --> 00:37:11.370
There's different possibilities,

00:37:11.370 --> 00:37:13.033
so we'll work on these things.

00:37:13.970 --> 00:37:18.260
We have another question in
the chat mostly for Shruti,

00:37:18.260 --> 00:37:21.693
because you went to two
different rounds of application.

00:37:22.880 --> 00:37:27.320
They wanted to know, is
it better to wait a turn?

00:37:27.320 --> 00:37:29.740
Like if you apply and then get a position

00:37:29.740 --> 00:37:32.129
that you could do better next time.

00:37:32.129 --> 00:37:33.850
Is it worth taking it?

00:37:33.850 --> 00:37:37.730
Or would it look bad similar
to like burning bridges

00:37:37.730 --> 00:37:39.900
if you decide to refuse this

00:37:39.900 --> 00:37:43.420
and then try to get a
better option next year?

00:37:43.420 --> 00:37:44.763
What's your take on that?

00:37:45.710 --> 00:37:47.707
- I think as long as, I don't know,

00:37:47.707 --> 00:37:49.180
you don't hit somebody over there

00:37:49.180 --> 00:37:51.413
you're not gonna like burn any bridges.

00:37:53.550 --> 00:37:55.260
Like when you...

00:37:55.260 --> 00:37:56.660
If you're called for an interview

00:37:56.660 --> 00:37:58.140
and you're not the first choice

00:37:58.140 --> 00:38:00.370
or not one of the top three choices,

00:38:00.370 --> 00:38:02.560
it doesn't mean that you are bad

00:38:02.560 --> 00:38:04.590
or you don't have the skills.

00:38:04.590 --> 00:38:06.800
It's just that you're not right now a fit

00:38:06.800 --> 00:38:09.250
for the particular direction

00:38:09.250 --> 00:38:10.950
that the university is looking at.

00:38:11.950 --> 00:38:13.193
So to me,

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:16.623
it doesn't show anything negative.

00:38:18.660 --> 00:38:22.103
That being said, I would say
strike when the iron is hot.

00:38:23.760 --> 00:38:25.360
There are some key papers

00:38:25.360 --> 00:38:28.510
that you're going to
publish in your career.

00:38:28.510 --> 00:38:33.050
When those hot papers come
out, apply for a position.

00:38:33.050 --> 00:38:34.750
You need to be out there.

00:38:34.750 --> 00:38:37.250
And that's when you're
gonna be more popular.

00:38:37.250 --> 00:38:39.740
Don't wait for like two more years,

00:38:39.740 --> 00:38:42.890
because after two more
years you may not have

00:38:42.890 --> 00:38:44.410
another hot paper again.

00:38:44.410 --> 00:38:47.533
And then your chances are gonna be lower.

00:38:50.050 --> 00:38:51.653
- You're talking about hot paper.

00:38:52.863 --> 00:38:54.190
Can you both talk about

00:38:54.190 --> 00:38:56.740
how can you strengthen your application?

00:38:56.740 --> 00:38:58.580
Do you need to have teaching experience?

00:38:58.580 --> 00:38:59.700
Do you need to have grants?

00:38:59.700 --> 00:39:03.490
What are the things you can
do as senior grad students

00:39:03.490 --> 00:39:07.430
or as a postdoc to have
a really strong case

00:39:07.430 --> 00:39:09.483
for the university to hire you?

00:39:15.120 --> 00:39:18.560
- Yeah. So I think of
course, first of all,

00:39:18.560 --> 00:39:22.020
we need to have a good
research background.

00:39:22.020 --> 00:39:24.800
So that's the foundation
for everything I believe.

00:39:24.800 --> 00:39:26.630
And the on top of that,

00:39:26.630 --> 00:39:31.630
I think that if you have some
experience getting some grants

00:39:31.680 --> 00:39:33.980
as PhD students or postdocs,

00:39:33.980 --> 00:39:36.270
I think that will add points to it.

00:39:36.270 --> 00:39:38.230
But I believe in terms of

00:39:40.160 --> 00:39:41.840
in the community of quantum information,

00:39:41.840 --> 00:39:44.160
I think there are not many chances

00:39:44.160 --> 00:39:46.840
for postdocs to write grants, I believe.

00:39:46.840 --> 00:39:50.670
Because I know that for
biology and medical schools

00:39:50.670 --> 00:39:52.870
postdocs can't apply for grants.

00:39:52.870 --> 00:39:56.163
But that's not very common
in quantum information area.

00:39:57.550 --> 00:39:58.383
Yeah.

00:39:58.383 --> 00:40:01.510
But on the other hand, if you can

00:40:01.510 --> 00:40:03.490
at least direct something

00:40:03.490 --> 00:40:07.250
or participant in some grant preparation,

00:40:07.250 --> 00:40:09.000
I think that would be very helpful.

00:40:13.330 --> 00:40:14.350
- Yeah.

00:40:14.350 --> 00:40:16.260
I did not have any grants.

00:40:16.260 --> 00:40:18.610
I've never applied for a grant.

00:40:18.610 --> 00:40:22.420
I didn't get any awards
or anything like that

00:40:22.420 --> 00:40:26.143
when I was a grad student or
early in my postdoc career.

00:40:31.990 --> 00:40:33.663
You have to basically show that,

00:40:34.620 --> 00:40:37.140
a, the research you're doing is important.

00:40:37.140 --> 00:40:39.940
It's changing the direction of something,

00:40:39.940 --> 00:40:41.553
whatever field you're in.

00:40:43.680 --> 00:40:47.580
Not only that, you can
pump out that new research

00:40:47.580 --> 00:40:49.763
for the next however many years.

00:40:51.190 --> 00:40:53.810
And you also have to
establish your independence

00:40:53.810 --> 00:40:54.790
from your advisor.

00:40:54.790 --> 00:40:58.293
That was the one important
advice that I was given.

00:41:01.120 --> 00:41:04.210
As a postdoc, there's not a...

00:41:04.210 --> 00:41:05.820
Again it depends upon your situation.

00:41:05.820 --> 00:41:07.830
You may have an opportunity
to do it, you may not.

00:41:07.830 --> 00:41:09.260
Experimentalist, it's a little bit hard,

00:41:09.260 --> 00:41:11.960
because it's the lab
that your advisor has.

00:41:11.960 --> 00:41:15.150
So you basically do what's
in the lab pretty much.

00:41:15.150 --> 00:41:17.150
For a theorist it's a little bit easier.

00:41:17.150 --> 00:41:18.870
You can come up...

00:41:18.870 --> 00:41:19.890
All you need is a paper and pen,

00:41:19.890 --> 00:41:21.900
so you can do whatever
the heck you want to.

00:41:21.900 --> 00:41:23.750
So in my case, it was you know...

00:41:25.420 --> 00:41:28.990
I was able to demonstrate that my research

00:41:28.990 --> 00:41:33.250
is different and
different from my advisor,

00:41:33.250 --> 00:41:36.110
and that I'll be able to
continue that research

00:41:37.360 --> 00:41:39.230
into the future.

00:41:39.230 --> 00:41:42.935
So that's what I would say is important.

00:41:42.935 --> 00:41:47.935
- And I would also say that
maybe applying for a fellowship

00:41:48.648 --> 00:41:51.790
to be a postdoctoral fellow.

00:41:51.790 --> 00:41:54.750
It's a process that would
somehow also teach us

00:41:54.750 --> 00:41:55.980
how to write a grant,

00:41:55.980 --> 00:41:58.811
because it's a short version of a grant

00:41:58.811 --> 00:42:00.690
that you have to think about

00:42:00.690 --> 00:42:04.154
how that project will be developed.

00:42:04.154 --> 00:42:07.810
What are the scope, objectives?

00:42:07.810 --> 00:42:11.950
Then you go through a
process of selection.

00:42:11.950 --> 00:42:14.270
So this whole experience somehow

00:42:14.270 --> 00:42:17.690
it starts to teach us
how to write a grant.

00:42:17.690 --> 00:42:20.250
And if we get that,

00:42:20.250 --> 00:42:22.400
that is something that is quite valuable.

00:42:22.400 --> 00:42:26.480
It's seen as a sign of independence.

00:42:26.480 --> 00:42:31.480
That you are able to search for a grant

00:42:31.560 --> 00:42:36.560
and eventually get approval.

00:42:37.200 --> 00:42:40.880
So I would definitely advise

00:42:40.880 --> 00:42:43.510
like trying to apply for a fellowship.

00:42:43.510 --> 00:42:48.510
It's something that would
definitely advance your abilities

00:42:49.220 --> 00:42:53.863
and will give you much
better chances to get hired.

00:42:55.065 --> 00:42:58.280
- Just one more thing that I
forgot to say earlier was also

00:42:59.560 --> 00:43:03.500
try to give as many talks as possible,

00:43:03.500 --> 00:43:06.510
including try to get invited talks.

00:43:06.510 --> 00:43:07.740
They do look at that.

00:43:07.740 --> 00:43:12.070
They want to know that
your research is important.

00:43:12.070 --> 00:43:14.540
That people will actually request

00:43:14.540 --> 00:43:15.970
for you to come and talk about your work

00:43:15.970 --> 00:43:18.570
and rather than you just
applying to talk somewhere.

00:43:21.500 --> 00:43:23.330
- And regarding fellowship,

00:43:23.330 --> 00:43:25.990
there's more and more
big centers, institute,

00:43:25.990 --> 00:43:29.070
and industry providing
postdoctoral fellowship.

00:43:29.070 --> 00:43:32.190
I know YQI has the YQI fellowships

00:43:32.190 --> 00:43:33.520
that actually are open right now.

00:43:33.520 --> 00:43:34.420
So you can apply.

00:43:34.420 --> 00:43:35.760
You have until November 15.

00:43:35.760 --> 00:43:38.520
I'm just putting it out
there if you're interested.

00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:42.210
But there's also a ton of
similar programs everywhere.

00:43:42.210 --> 00:43:43.480
So these are like,

00:43:43.480 --> 00:43:45.680
we call them like gold star postdocs.

00:43:45.680 --> 00:43:47.380
It's similar to your regular postdoc,

00:43:47.380 --> 00:43:48.900
but they have a little bit of shine on it.

00:43:48.900 --> 00:43:50.070
So that might help you.

00:43:50.070 --> 00:43:51.570
It's not gonna do everything,

00:43:51.570 --> 00:43:53.530
but it might like shine a little bit

00:43:53.530 --> 00:43:55.150
of varnish in your application.

00:43:55.150 --> 00:43:56.450
That's also something you can do

00:43:56.450 --> 00:43:58.097
that might help a little bit.

00:44:00.759 --> 00:44:05.003
So looking at the last
few questions that I have.

00:44:06.070 --> 00:44:08.470
We're getting inviting talks.

00:44:08.470 --> 00:44:10.430
Like how do you proceed for that?

00:44:10.430 --> 00:44:11.430
Like is it similar to...?

00:44:11.430 --> 00:44:13.850
We have a question in the
chat about visiting scholars

00:44:13.850 --> 00:44:16.770
and visiting researcher during
PhD and during postdocs.

00:44:16.770 --> 00:44:20.030
Is that something that
you've done in the past?

00:44:20.030 --> 00:44:23.653
Or is it something that we
haven't seen, you haven't done?

00:44:26.810 --> 00:44:28.590
- I personally haven't done any

00:44:28.590 --> 00:44:33.330
like visiting scholars or
researchers and in places.

00:44:33.330 --> 00:44:36.757
So the reason is because
I'm an experimentalist.

00:44:37.742 --> 00:44:42.742
Specifically I need to focus
on my experiments, projects.

00:44:43.020 --> 00:44:44.630
And we have already built up

00:44:44.630 --> 00:44:46.160
that capability in my own group.

00:44:46.160 --> 00:44:49.453
So there's no point for
me to visit another group.

00:44:50.303 --> 00:44:54.610
But I do see a lot of theorists
visiting other institutes.

00:44:54.610 --> 00:44:56.650
Especially in many cases,

00:44:56.650 --> 00:45:00.650
I find it fruitful to have
a theoretical researcher

00:45:00.650 --> 00:45:03.510
to visit experiment group.

00:45:03.510 --> 00:45:08.123
And in many cases I find that
discussion is very helpful.

00:45:10.800 --> 00:45:12.336
- Thank you.

00:45:12.336 --> 00:45:16.200
And then I think one last thing
that I want to talk about.

00:45:16.200 --> 00:45:17.940
There was a point about Shruti.

00:45:17.940 --> 00:45:19.870
